Abstract
The incorporation of polyoxometalates (POMs) in metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) with host−guest structure have proven to be effective strategy to rational design of heterogeneous catalysis. In this study, the Keggin-type $\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ composite catalysts $(\mathrm{PM}\mathbf{o}_{12}$ , $\mathrm{PW}_{12}$ and $\mathrm{SiW}_{12})$ are synthesized for nitrogen fixation reaction without sacrificial agents at room temperature in the first time. The $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ molecules are encapsulated in smaller cavities of MIL-101(Cr) by solvothermal method and in larger cavities by impregnation method, respectively. Solvothermal synthesized catalyst has a performance of $75.56~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}}{\mathrm{g}^{-1}}_{\mathrm{cat}}$ and TOF value of $1.95~\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ , which are about 10 and 88 times than that of $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ . The excellent performance is ascribed to the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ and MIL- $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ . The MIL-101(Cr) adsorbs a large amount of $\Nu_{2}$ and generates sufficiently photogenerated electrons under sunlight irradiation, and electrons quickly transfer to the $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ through hydrogen bonds. Moreover, the agglomeration effect of the homogeneous catalyst $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ is weakened due to encapsulation with more exposed active sites. This work provides a feasible route to design and synthesize nanocomposite materials with exceptional performance for photocatalytic nitrogen fixation.
Keywords: POM, MOF, Nitrogen fixation; Hydrogen bond
## 1. Introduction
Ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)$ is the most productive chemical product in the world, and it is mainly used to synthesize nitrogen fertilizer. $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is also a promising energy carrier to store and transport renewable hydrogen. Photocatalytic nitrogen fixation (PNF), as a green, sustainable, safe, and non-polluting process, has been widely studied for ammonia synthesis instead of energy-intensive and high- $\mathrm{.CO}_{2}$ -emmision Haber-Bosch method process (15–25 MPa, $300{-}550\quad^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ [1-3]. However, high efficiency photocatalysts for industrial production still need to be further studied. The hydrogen source in PNF is $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ basically, however, the solubility of $\Nu_{2}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is very lower and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution is a competitive reaction of $\Nu_{2}$ reduction. Therefore, it is necessary to design the photocatalysts with a large specific surface area and nitrogen active sites.
The Keggin-type polyoxometalates (POMs), centered on inorganic ions (Si, P) and surrounded by metal ions (Mo, W), are a class of soluble anionic oxide clusters based on metal ions in high oxidation states [4-6]. And the POMs, due to the excellent stability, abundant catalytic sites, peripheral metal regulation and multi-electron storage structure, are an ideal photocatalyst. In addition, the density functional theory (DFT) theoretical calculations of Liu [7, 8] and Zheng [9] jointly reveal the same result. Based on the analysis of the calculated adsorption energy of gas molecules, molecule bond length and the nature bond orbital calculation charges, reduced Keggin-type POMs have surprising reactivity in the nitrogen reduction reaction due to activation of the inert ${\mathrm{N}}{\equiv}{\mathrm{N}}$ molecule. However, Wang [10] and Chen [11] reported that the ammonia yield of $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}$ was $0.43~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\cdot\mathbf{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\mathrm{cat}}$ and that of $\mathrm{PW}_{12}$ was $4.60~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}$ in the actual PNF reaction, which is due to low specific surface area and agglomeration effect. In addition, POMs are difficult to recycle due to its excellent solubility [12-14]. Therefore, in this work, Keggin-type POMs was loaded into a suitable carrier as the active site in PNF reaction.
Porous tri-dimensional (3D) metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are regarded nowadays as an excellent type of material as their regular large porous structures and efficient light absorption capacity make them prime candidates to act as support for active chemical species in the photocatalytic reaction system [15-17]. The $\mathsf{P O M@M O F}$ material combines the advantage of POMs and MOFs, which shows excellent catalytic performance and has been widely used in organic catalytic reaction [12-14]. As one of the representatives of MOFs, MIL-101(Cr) $(\mathrm{[Cr_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}(O H)(\upmu_{3}{-}O)(B D C)_{3}]}$ , synthesized by fluorine-free method) has excellent chemical stability, huge specific surface area and good light absorption ability [18-20]. However, the catalytic performance of MIL-101(Cr) in PNF reaction is close to $0\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathbf{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}$ due to the lack of catalytic active sites [21]. At the same time, MIL-101(Cr) owns two types of quasi-spherical mesoporous cages [22-24], compared with the diameter of $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the Keggin-type POMs, the smaller cavity with 1 nm hexagonal window and $2\mathrm{nm}$ pore size can accommodate one Keggintype POM and restrict its escape, while the larger cavities with $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ pentagonal windows and $2.6\mathrm{nm}$ pore size can accommodate several Keggin-type POMs. Therefore, MIL-101(Cr) is a suitable carrier for loading Keggin-type POMs for PNF reaction.
In this paper, $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ was applied for PNF reaction and studied in detail as the active site. The X-MIL-101(Cr)-Y (X represents the $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ amount and Y represents the synthetic methods) composites were prepared by impregnation (I) and solvothermal (S) method to test the catalytic performance. Compared with the performance of 7.71 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}$ for $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ and the performance of $6.17~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}$ for MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ , the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ production rate of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I and $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ is $33.93~\upmu\mathrm{mol\cdoth^{-1}\cdot g^{-1}}\mathrm{cat}$ and $75.56~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\cdot\mathrm{h^{-1}}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\mathrm{cat}},$ respectively. The great promotion of catalytic efficiency is attributed to the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ and MIL-101(Cr).
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
All reagents were used as received without further purification in this work. The chromium nitrate $(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ , $98\%$ , terephthalic acid $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}B D C}$ , $99\%$ ) and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ , Ndimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ ) were purchased from Energy Chemical. The phosphomolybdic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}P M o_{12}O_{40}}$ , $99\%$ ), phosphotungstic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ , $99\%$ ) and silicotungstic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40},$ $99\%$ ) were purchased from Aladdin chemistry Co. Deionized water was purified through a Millipore system.
## 2.2 General methods
IR spectra were taken on a Perkin-Elmer spectrum One FT-IR spectrometer in the $4000{\sim}400~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ region with KBr pellets. X-ray diffraction (XRD) data were obtained from an AXS D8 ADVANCE A25 with $\mathrm{{Cu-Ka}}$ radiation $(40\mathrm{kV},40\mathrm{mA})$ ) of wavelength $0.154~\mathrm{nm}$ (Germany). SEM images and Energy Dispersive Spectrometer (EDS) were collected from the ZEISS Sigma 300 scanning electron microscope at an accelerating voltage of $30~\mathrm{kV}$ (Germany). TEM images were obtained from a FEI Talos 200S transmission electron microscope operated at $200\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kV}}$ . XPS measurements were performed on a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha X-ray photoelectron spectrometer using Al as the exciting source. The nitrogen physisorption Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) experiments were collected on a Mike ASAP2460 system. The absorbance data of spectrophotometer were measured on a Persee TU-19 UV-Vis spectrophotometer. The Ion chromatography (IC) was tested on a Thermo Fisher Aquion.
## 2.3 Catalysts synthesis
## 2.3.1 Synthesis of MIL-101(Cr)
A mixture of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(2\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (2 mmol) in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was sealed in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless autoclave after stirring for 30 minutes. The autoclave was heated to $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{h}$ followed by cooling to room temperature. The gray-green solid powder was obtained by centrifugation at 8000 RPM. Then wash three times with DMF and deionized water respectively. The product was vacuum dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ .
2.3.2 Synthesis of X-MIL-101(Cr)-I by impregnation method ( $\mathrm{T}=$ impregnation)
$0.5\mathrm{\g}$ of dry MIL-101 synthesized in autoclave as described above was suspended in a suitable concentration aqueous solution of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ ( $\mathrm{T}=50$ , 100, $250~\mathrm{mg}$ in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathit{j}}^{\rangle}$ ) under stirring for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature. The gray-green solid powder was obtained by centrifugation at 8000 RPM. Then wash three times with deionized water. The product was vacuum dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ .
2.3.3 Synthesis of X-MIL-101(Cr)-S by solvothermal method ( $\mathbf{\bar{S}}=\mathbf{\bar{\Lambda}}$ solvothermal)
A mixture of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (2 mmol), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (2 mmol) and a suitable amount of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaX}=50$ , 100, 200, $300~\mathrm{mg}$ ) in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was sealed in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless autoclave after stirring for 30 minutes. The autoclave was heated to $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ followed by cooling to room temperature. The gray-green solid powder was obtained by centrifugation at 8000 RPM. Then wash three times with DMF and deionized water respectively. The product was vacuum dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.4 Photocatalytic test
Photocatalytic tests were tested in a quartz reactor under UV-vis light irradiation by 300W Xenon lamp (PLS-SXE 300, Beijing Perfect Light Co., Ltd.) at $15\mathrm{~A~}$ lamp current. Typically, the catalyst powder $(30~\mathrm{mg})$ was dispersed in $100~\mathrm{mL}$ of ultrapure water by ultrasound for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the light source adjusts to $10\mathrm{cm}$ away from the mixing liquid level. Nitrogen source was offered through air or bubbling $\Nu_{2}$ . Before irradiation, the solution was stirred in darkness for $30\mathrm{min}$ with bubbling $\Nu_{2}$ to reach equilibration of $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption desorption on catalyst surface, and highly pure $\Nu_{2}$ was continuously bubbled with a dispersion rate of approximately $80~\mathrm{mL/min}$ through the experiment. The controlling experiments were performed in dark and Ar, respectively for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . The concentration of ammonia was measured by the Nessler's reagent method at the indicated time intervals.
## 2.5 Electrochemical measurement
The electrochemical measurements were performed on a CHI 660D electrochemical workstation (Shanghai CH Instruments, P. R. China) using a standard three-electrode configuration. A 300W Xe lamp (PLS-SXE300, Beijing Perfect Light Co., Ltd.) was used as light source. For preparing working electrode, catalyst powder was coated on a fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) substrate about $2\mathrm{cm}\times2.5$ cm square. Firstly, $5\mathrm{mg}$ of photocatalyst was dispersed in the mixture of $0.49~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol solution and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion solution $(5\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ , and the mixtures were ultrasonically scattered for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Subsequently, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of above suspension was coated on the FTO glass, after natural evaporation of ethanol and then dry at room temperature for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ under vacuum conditions. The catalyst coated FTO substrate was used as the working electrode, Pt plate was as counter electrode, and $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ was as the reference electrode. The potentials were converted to RHE scale via calibration with the following equation: E $(\mathrm{vs.\RHE})=\mathrm{E}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl)+0.197+0.059\timespH}$ . The electrolyte was 0.1 M ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution with bubbling $\Nu_{2}$ or Ar.
2.6 Isotope labeling experiment and NMR test.
The $^{15}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotopic labeling experiment was carried out using $^{15}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ as the feeding gas (Shanghai Research Institute of Chemical Industry C, 98 atom $\%^{15}\mathrm{N}$ ) with ethanol. After reaction for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of the reaction solution was filtered and acidized to $\mathrm{pH}\sim$ 3 by adding $0.5\mathrm{~M~H_{2}S O_{4}}$ , which was concentrated to $10~\mathrm{mL}$ . Afterwards, $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of the resulting solution was taken out and mixed with $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Sigma-Aldrich, $99.99\%$ . The mixed solution was performed $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance measurement ( $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR, Bruker Avance III $600\mathrm{MHz}$ ).
2.7 Turover frequency.
The performance of Photocatalytic reaction generating $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is further evaluated by the turnover frequency (TOF). Catalytic efficiency of the unit molar catalyst per unit time
was determined by the following formula:
$$
\mathrm{TON=\frac{n_{NH^{3}}}{n_{P O M}},\Delta\ T O F=\frac{T O N}{T i m e}}
$$
where TON is the turnover number, ${\mathfrak{n}}_{\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ is the number of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ moles produced, nPOM the molar amount of the POM in catalyst, and time (h) is the reaction time.
## $2.8\:\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ concentration measurement
The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}{}^{+}$ concentration was analyzed by Nessler's reagent method. Firstly, $100~\mathrm{mL}$ of the suspension was filtered through a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membrane filter and transferred to a 50 mL volumetric flask. Then, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of the potassium sodium tartrate solution and $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of Nessler's reagent was added to the same volumetric flask and mixed. Then the mixture was left to stand for $15~\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the concentration of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ was detected using a UV-vis spectrophotometer (Persee TU-1901) at $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ wavelength. The concentration absorbance curves were calibrated using a standard $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}C l}$ solution with a series of concentrations. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ detection of UV-vis absorption spectra and fitting curve is shown in Fig. S1.
## $2.9\:\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ concentration measurement
The $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ concentration was analyzed by Watt-Chrisp method. A mixture of $\mathrm{C}_{9}\mathrm{H}_{11}\mathrm{NO}$ $(5.99\ \mathrm{g})$ , HCl (concentrated, $30~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and ethanol $(300~\mathrm{mL})$ was used as a $\mathrm{\partial_{\ti}C_{9}H_{1i}N O}$ colorants. Firstly, $100~\mathrm{mL}$ of the suspension was filtered through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ membrane filter. Then, $5\mathrm{mL}$ of the filtrate and $5\mathrm{mL}$ of the $\mathrm{p{-}C_{9}H_{11}N O}$ colorants were mixed and stirred for 15 minutes at room temperature. Finally, the concentration of $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ was detected using a UV-vis spectrophotometer (Persee TU-1901) at $455~\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength. The concentration absorbance curves were calibrated using a standard ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ solution with a series of concentrations. The $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ detection of UV-vis absorption spectra and fitting curve is shown in Fig. S2.
## 3. Results and Discussion
The three composites supported with the Keggin-type POMs $(\mathrm{PM}\mathbf{o}_{12}$ , $\mathrm{PW}_{12}$ and $\mathrm{SiW}_{12})$ ) were firstly prepared by solvothermal method and proved the successful synthesis by XRD (Fig. S3). The PNF reaction performance of the three catalysts was investigated under pure water (Fig. S4). It was found that the reaction activity of tungsten-POM supported catalyst was improved, but molybdenum-POM did not change the catalytic performance. At the same time, $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ supported catalyst has higher catalytic efficiency than $\mathrm{PW}_{12}$ . It was owing to that $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ has stronger electron acceptance ability and hydrophobicity than $\mathrm{PW}_{12}$ [25, 26], which is conducive to the rapid separation of photogenerated carriers and suppress the hydrogen evolution reaction. Therefore, $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ was applied for PNF reaction and studied in detail as the active site. The X-MIL-101(Cr)-Y (X represents the $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ amount and $\mathrm{Y}$ represents the synthetic methods) were prepared (Scheme 1) by impregnation (I) and solvothermal (S) method respectively (See synthesis details in experimental section).
The chemical structures of the materials were characterized by Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy and X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns. As shown in Fig. 1a and S5, for $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ , the FT-IR spectrum showed the peaks at $980\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , $925\mathrm{cm}^{-}$ 1 $,885\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and $790\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which attributed to the vibrations in the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{W=O}$ , Si-O, $\mathrm{W\mathrm{-}O_{b}}$ -W and ${\mathrm{W-O}}_{\mathrm{c}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{W}$ bonds, respectively [27, 28]. The typical vibrational bands of the MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ assigned to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ , O-C, $C{=}0$ , C-H in terephthalic acid centering at $1625~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , $1515~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , $1400~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and $756~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , respectively [29, 30]. Kapteijn et al. [31] found indications for the partial exchange of W by $\mathrm{Cr}$ in the $\mathrm{PW}_{12}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ that observing the shift of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{W=O}$ band $982\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ to $963~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . However, the change did not occur in this work and the FT-IR spectrum of the composite material exhibited both the typical vibrational absorption peaks of the MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ and $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ , indicating that the integrity of the polyoxometalates structure in the MOF. Concurrently, the spectroscopic redshift phenomenon at $1515\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $980\mathrm{cm}^{\cdot}$ 1 of composites was attributed to the formation of the hydrogen bond between MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ and $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ [32, 33].
Fig. 1b and S6 shows the XRD patterns of simulated MIL-101(Cr), as-prepared MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ , $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ and X-MIL-101(Cr)-Y. The position of diffraction peaks patterns of as-prepared MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ and X-MIL-101(Cr)-Y corresponds well with simulated MIL-101(Cr), confirming the formation of pure phase MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ . Concurrently, the diffraction peaks of POM were undetected in the patterns of as-prepared X-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})–\mathrm{Y}$ , showing that the few POM molecules are compounded and dispersed in the pores of the MOF instead of attached to the surface. But, for physically mixed POM and MIL-101(Cr), the diffraction peak of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ still retains because the POM is not dispersed into the pores of the MOF (Fig. S7). It is worth noting that the position of weakened diffraction peak intensity for $\scriptstyle100-\mathrm{MIL}-101(\mathrm{Cr})-\mathrm{I}$ and for 100-MIL-101(Cr)- S is different. The diffraction peak intensity at $2\uptheta=5{-}10{\textrm{\textperthousand}}$ for $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}101({\mathrm{Cr}}){\mathrm{-S}}$ is weakened. This indicating that the slight structure distortion of the MOFs after the inclusion of the POMs in the pores and interaction of the POMs with the MOFs framework. The diffraction peaks of $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\mathrm{-I}}$ at $26\ =\ 3{-}5^{\circ}$ almost disappeared, which assigned to a structure where the POMs resided in the mesopores of MOFs, shown in Scheme 2.
$\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption experiments were performed to research the distribution of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in MIL-101(Cr) in detail. As shown in Fig. 2a and Table S1, the BET surface area of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I, 250-MIL-101(Cr)-I, 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S and 300-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ decreases from $2923.61\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of MIL-101(Cr) to $1908.88~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , 1644.49 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $2138.11{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $1667.23\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. And the total volume in pores of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I, 250-MIL-101(Cr)-I, 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S and 300-MIL-101(Cr)-S decrease from $1.307\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of MIL-101(Cr) to $1.089\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $0.796\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $1.112\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.671\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The BET surface area and the total volume in pores decrease as the amount of POM increase. The part of the cavity of MIL-101(Cr) is occupied due to the $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ supported, resulting in a decrease in nitrogen adsorption capacity. Nevertheless, nitrogen molecules have a relatively small diameter (0.364 $\mathrm{nm})^{[34]}$ and can still be adsorbed into the filled pores as well as the unfilled pores, providing sufficient raw materials for nitrogen fixation reaction. The BET surface areas of MIL-101(Cr)-I were lower than that of MIL-101(Cr)-S because the larger pores in MIL-101(Cr)-I were occupied by POM. Exactly, Fig. 2b intuitively shows the distribution of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in MIL-101(Cr). Compared with MIL-101(Cr), the content of larger cavities in 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I is significantly reduced, while 100-MIL-101(Cr)- S is the opposite. It is owing to that the smaller cavity with $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ hexagonal window can accommodate one $1.2\ \mathrm{nm\POM}$ ion and restrict its escape during the process of solvothermal synthesis (Scheme 2). While the larger cavities with $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ pentagonal windows allow the POM ions wandering freely in the process of impregnation synthesis.
Thermal stability and decomposition patterns of MIL-101(Cr) and composites with $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ were assessed by thermo gravimetric analysis (TGA), and the DTG and TG curves were shown in Fig. 2c and S8. The MIL-101(Cr) lost the hydroxylic groups in the range of $210{-}350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then the framework decomposed between $360{-}600\ {^{\circ}}\mathrm{C}.$ ， which is consistent with that reported in the literature [35, 36]. The hydroxylic groups removal temperature and framework decomposition temperature of the composites shifted to the high temperature region, which is attributed to the formation of hydrogen bonds between $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ and MIL-101 $\mathrm{(Cr)}$ and consistent with FT-IR result.
In order to investigate the content of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ supported in three photocatalysts and defects of metal clusters in MOF, inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) were performed. The result is shown in Table 1. The content of $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ in three photocatalysts is close to the theoretical value of $21.78\%$ , indicating that metal cluster defects are not existence. The content of POM in MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , 100- MIL-101(Cr)-I and 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S is $0\mathrm{g/g_{MOF}}$ , $0.1953\mathrm{g/g_{\mathrm{MOF}}}$ and $0.1254\mathrm{g/g_{MOF}}$ respectively. The amount of POM in 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S is only a little less than 100- MIL-101(Cr)-I.
The morphology and particle size of the catalyst is a factor affecting the catalytic performance. The morphology features of three materials were observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) in Fig. 3. The $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in the pores did not change the morphology of the MOF, and these catalysts all had a tetradecahedral structure. The particle size of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I synthesized by impregnation method is consistent with MIL-101(Cr), both of which are $200{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ . But the particle size of composites synthesized by solvothermal method is reduced due to the template effect of POM ion during the formation of MIL-101(Cr) [37, 38]. With the increase of POM in the synthesis process, the particle sizes of 50-MIL-101(Cr)-S, 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S and 200-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ are reduced from $200{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ of MIL-101(Cr) to $150{-}250~\mathrm{nm}$ , $100{-}200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $50{\cdot}150\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the surface of the photocatalyst after loading $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ did not change significantly, indicating that POM was evenly dispersed in the pores rather than agglomerated on the surface of the MOF. Moreover, the corresponding energy-dispersive spectrometry (EDS) (Fig. S10-S12) demonstrate the uniform distribution of all elements within materials, further proving the $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ has been evenly loaded into the MIL-101(Cr).
In order to further investigate the microstructure and element distribution of 100- MIL-101(Cr)-I and 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S, transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-angle annular dark field (HAADF) were performed. As shown in Fig. 4a-4e, no lattice fringes were observed by the high-resolution transmission microscopy. The HAADF images are shown in Fig. 4e and 4i, the element mapping images are shown in Fig. 4f-4h and 4j-4l. The distribution of Cr, Si and W are uniform, which proves that $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ is uniformly loaded into MIL-101(Cr) and the particle condensate of POM is not formed. These results are consistent with the SEM and EDS analysis.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed to analyze the valence state of each element in photocatalysts, and the total spectrum is shown in Fig. S13a. The O 1s XPS results is shown in Fig. 5a, the peak at $532.23\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to the -COOH of the ligand in the MOF. At the same time, the binding energy of W-O-W at $531.23\mathrm{eV}$ can be detected after POM loading. The detection of W-O-H[28, 39] at $533.83\mathrm{~~eV}$ indicates that the POM located in the cavity is connected to the MOF through hydrogen bonds, which is also consistent with the conclusion of the existence of hydrogen bonds obtained by IR and DTG. The Cr $2\mathsf{P}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}2\ensuremath{\mathrm{P}}_{1/2}$ binding energy can be divided at 577.79 and $587.15\mathrm{eV}$ [40-42], representing the peak position of the $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ in Fig. 5b. The binding energy of Cr slightly shifts to high energy after POM supported, showing that Cr element tends to lose electrons and transfer electrons to POM through hydrogen bonds. From the spectra of the C 1s in Fig. S13b, the peaks of C-C, C-O and $0.0{\cdot}=0$ are located at $284.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , $285.84\mathrm{~eV}$ and $288.83\mathrm{~eV}$ . The spectra of the $\mathrm{Si}2\mathsf{p}$ and W 4f were shown in Fig. S13c and S13d, the peaks at $102.15~\mathrm{eV},35.77~\mathrm{eV}$ and $37.89\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Si-O, W $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and W $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ in 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I and 100-MIL-101(Cr)- S, respectively [43-45].
In the process of photocatalytic reduction of $\Nu_{2}$ , the driving force of the reaction is determined by the effective absorption and utilization of light energy by the catalyst, which is another factor affecting the catalytic performance. The UV/Vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) of catalysts were conducted to explore the response region from $220{-}850\mathrm{nm}$ and showed in Fig. 5c. MIL-101(Cr) absorbs light with wavelengths below $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ , showing the advantages as a carrier for PNF reaction. Meanwhile, the supported POM enhances the absorption of visible light by the composites. As can be seen in Fig. 5d, the band gap of MIL-101(Cr), 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I, 100-MIL-101(Cr)- S and $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ is $2.95\mathrm{eV}$ , $3.00\mathrm{eV}$ , $2.89\mathrm{eV}$ and $3.19\mathrm{eV}$ respectively. The narrow band gap of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S can promote the effective utilization of sunlight and is also closer to the ideal band gap value of nitrogen fixation photocatalysts [46-48], which is more conducive to the photocatalytic performance.
The photocatalytic activity of X-MIL-101(Cr)-Y was explored for $\Nu_{2}$ fixation in water without a sacrificial agent under air (Fig. 6a and 6b). The 50-MIL-101(Cr)-I, 100- MIL-101(Cr)-I and 250-MIL-101(Cr)-I achieve a $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ yield of $23.13~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ cat, $33.93~\upmu\mathrm{mol\cdoth^{-1}\cdot g^{-1}}\mathrm{cat}$ and $27.76~\mathrm{\upmumol\cdoth^{-1}\cdot g^{-1}}\mathrm{{cat}},$ respectively. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ production rate of $50\mathrm{-MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S},$ , 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S, $200{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\mathrm{-S}}$ and 300-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ is $47.81\ \mathrm{\textmumol}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\textcircled{c}\cdot\mathrm{~7}5.56\ \upmu m o l}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Lambda}_{\mathrm{cat}},38.55\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Lambda}_{\mathrm{cat}}\ a_{0}^{-1},$ nd 15.42 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat},$ respectively. The catalytic performance of the photocatalysts synthesized by the two methods showed a volcanic shape with the increase of the POM loading content. This is because excessive $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ loaded block the pores of the MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ , hindering the adsorption of $\Nu_{2}$ by the composites, which is consistent with the BET analysis results. On the contrary, the catalytic potential of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ cannot be fully utilized. The $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\mathrm{-I}}$ and 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S with the best POM loading for the two synthesis methods was determined through the performance test, and the performance of $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ is $104.86~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}}{\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}}_{\mathrm{cat}}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ much higher than $\mathrm{PMoV}@Z_{\mathrm{IF}-67}$ (ethanol as hole sacrificial agent, converted ammonia yield of $49.67~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}$ under $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) [49], which was in a leading level compared with the productivity of other POM-based catalysts (Details were shown in Table S2).
The measurement results of ion chromatography (IC) for 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S is
$70.37~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}$ in Fig. S14, which is consistent with Nessler's reagent method. Meanwhile, the photocatalytic performance of MIL-101(Cr) is $6.17\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\cdoth^{-1}\cdot g^{-1}}_{\mathrm{cat}}.$ Considering the effect of $\mathsf{p H}$ on the PNF reaction, neutral $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ was used for the catalytic performance test. And the photocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ is $7.71\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}.$ In addition, the turnover frequency (TOF) was calculated to further evaluate the performance of the catalyst [10,50]. The calculated TOF values of $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ , 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I, 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S are $0.0222~\mathrm{{h^{-1}}}$ , $0.597{\mathrm{~h}}^{-1}$ , and $1.95~\mathrm{~h^{-1}}$ , respectively. These results show that 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S display best photocatalytic $\Nu_{2}$ fixation performance.
The UV-vis DRS test of the catalyst shows that $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ does not respond in the visible light region. The catalytic performance of MIL-101( $\mathrm{\DeltaCr)}$ , $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ and 100- MIL-101(Cr)-S were tested in the visible light region to further determine the catalytic sites and the transfer process of photogenerated electrons for 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S. As shown in Fig. 6c, the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ production rate of $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ is $49.34\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-}$ $^{1}\mathrm{{cat};}$ the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ production rate of MIL-101(Cr) is $6.17\ \mathrm{\upmumol\cdoth^{-1}\cdot g^{-1}\Phi_{c a t},N H_{4}^{+}}$ products are hardly detected for $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ . And the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ yield did not change significantly in the presence of the hole sacrificial agent (ethanol) (Fig.S15). These results show that the synergistic effect of the MIL- $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ and $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in the reaction is the key to improve the performance of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S. The $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ is the catalytic active site of the reaction and the electrons that MIL-101(Cr) is excited under sunlight irradiation transfer to $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ through hydrogen bonds. This is consistent with the Cr 2P XPS analysis.
The formation of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ products was not detected in the control comparison experiments of these photocatalysts in dark and argon (Fig. 6d). The $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ isotopic labeling experiments were further conducted to confirm the detected $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ originated from the PNF reaction (Fig. S16). It is worth noting that the performance of 100-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ is much higher than other materials, combined with the structural characterization of the above composite materials, which is attributed to, 1) the unfilled pores in the MOF adsorb a large amount of $\Nu_{2}$ ; 2) MOF generates enough photogenerated electrons under light excitation and is quickly transferred and stored in $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ through hydrogen bond; 3) the $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ confined in a smaller cavity avoids the effect of agglomeration, thereby retaining more active sites. The lower performance of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I is due to the agglomeration of multiple $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in the larger cavities of MIL-101(Cr), which decreases the content of effective reaction active sites. The performance of pure $\mathrm{Na_{4}S i W_{12}O_{40}}$ is much lower than composites due to the agglomeration in the solution, weak light absorption performance and lack of ability to capture ${\bf N}_{2}$ . Although MIL-101(Cr) has strong light absorption capacity, showing extremely low ammonia yield due to lacking of sufficient active center, which is consistent with the conclusion of Li [21].
To appraise practical application performance, the stability and durability of photocatalysts are tested. The $\Nu_{2}$ reduction activity of photocatalysts does not change significantly after 5 cycles (Fig. 6e and 6f). The IR and XRD of photocatalysts were further measured after cycle (Fig. S17 and S18), and find that their structure does not notable change, indicating that the photocatalysts show good photochemical stability and durability for fixing $\Nu_{2}$ . In order to research the fixation pathway of nitrogen, hydrazine was determined by the classical Watt-Chrisp method. $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ was not detected in the reaction solution after irradiation on 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S, the result shows that the PNF pathway was distal pathways, which is consistent with the density functional theory theoretical calculations of Liu [8].
The electronic separation transfer efficiency was measured by photoluminescent (PL) spectra (Fig. 7a). It is clear that MIL-101(Cr) have obvious emission peaks at about $352{\-}410\ \mathrm{nm}$ by reason of the quick recombination of electrons and holes. After the combination of MIL-101(Cr) and $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ , the composites occur rapid fluorescence quenching and the intensity of fluorescence absorption peak decreases, which is the result of the valid separation of electron–hole pairs and rapid electron transfer.
The rapid separation and transfer of electrons is required by the catalyst to exhibit high photocatalytic performance. An important factor affecting the efficiency of electron transfer is the electron-transfer impedance. Electro-chemical impedance spectra (EIS) are performed to explore the charge-transport efficiency of the photocatalysts (Fig. 7b). The arc diameter of 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S is smaller than that of MIL-101(Cr) and 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I, showing that the 100-MIL-101(Cr)-S has lower interface resistance and higher the electron-hole pair separation and charge transfer efficiency, which may be due to the formation of more extensive hydrogen bond network. In order to further verify the results of the EIS experiment, the transient photocurrent-time curve of the photocatalysts was measured. As shown in Fig. 7c and S19, compared with the photocurrent under argon, 100-MIL-101(Cr)-I and 100-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ expend more photoelectrons due to the more violent nitrogen fixation reaction, making them exhibit a weaker photocurrent under air. These fully indicate that the $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ supported effectively improves the separation and transfer of photogenerated
carriers in MIL-101(Cr).
Combined with the structural characteristics and performance of these photocatalysts, the nitrogen fixation reaction mechanism (Scheme 3) of $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\mathrm{-S}}$ is summarized. The BET test result shows that the $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\mathrm{-S}}$ still retains sufficient pores, allowing it to adsorb sufficient $\Nu_{2}$ , so as to fully contact the $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in the dispersed pores, and together with water as the raw material for the nitrogen reduction reaction. The UV-vis DRS and catalytic performance test result indicate that the $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ is the catalytic active site for PNF reaction. And combined with FT-IR, TGA, XPS and PL test results, it shows that the photogenerated electrons transfer to $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ from MIL-101(Cr) through hydrogen bonds. Under sunlight irradiation, MIL-101(Cr) is excited to produce photogenerated carriers. The electrons on the MIL- $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ conduction band (Fig. S20) $(0.54\mathrm{V})$ are transferred to the valence band $(3.12\mathrm{V})$ of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}[28]$ through hydrogen bond and combined with the holes. Finally, the electrons gather on the conduction band (-0.07 V) of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ to generate $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ through $\Nu_{2}$ reduction reaction, and the holes gather on the valence band (3.49 V) of MIL-101(Cr) to form $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ through OH- oxidation reaction.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, three typical Keggin-type POMs were successfully used for the synthesis of $\mathrm{POM}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ and the loading of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ in MIL-101(Cr) was investigated in detail. A series of photocatalyst of $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ supported in different cavities in the MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ were synthesized by controlling the synthesis method. These composites have been experimentally confirmed as an efficient photocatalytic for nitrogen fixation and transformed from homogeneous $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ to heterogeneous catalyst. The 100-MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr}){\mathrm{-I}}$ and $100{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ among them, achieved a high $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ yield of 33.93 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{cat}$ and $75.56~\upmu\mathrm{mol\cdoth^{-1}\cdot g^{-1}}_{\mathrm{cat}},$ had a TOF values of $0.597~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and $1.95~\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ which are much higher than $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ . The excellent performance is ascribed to the synergistic effect of the porous MIL-101(Cr) and $\mathrm{SiW}_{12}$ . Previous reports on POMbased PNF materials generally believed that photogenerated electrons were generated by POM under irradiation and transferred to carriers [10, 49, 51]. In this paper, the catalytic performance of POM $@$ MOFs materials with POM as the active site in the PNF reaction is explored and investigated for the first time without sacrificial agents, which provides a new strategy for the design of POM-based PNF materials. We anticipate that more high-activity POM@MOFs will be obtained by regulating the active site.
